Chapter: CritterPlanSpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Frame
  cites Safari chapter Critter
  cites Safari chapter Pond
  cites Safari chapter SafariCritter
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter DepthSort
  cites Safari chapter CritterPlan
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 CritterPlan: which animals are close enough to be worth placing, and in what
 order they go into the sort.

 TWO REACHES, AND THEY ARE DIFFERENT NUMBERS ON PURPOSE. Farm animals stop being
 collected three chain segments out; the corner pair and the ducks carry to five.
 A herd is many small billboards and a corner creature is one large one, so the
 far herd is not worth the walk while the far elephant still is. Grading both
 cutoffs from either side is what says there are two rules -- a single reach
 would agree with this everywhere except at chain entries three and four.

 A DUCK IS AUTHORED IN THE CORNER FRAME AND MAPS RAW, with no half-width shift,
 which the chapter calls out as the thing easy to get wrong: its `cu` is the
 bottom-left across directly, so the pond's negative `cu` sits left of the LEFT
 EDGE rather than left of the centre line. Getting it wrong slides the whole pond
 half a lane and still looks like a pond. Here it shows up as ducks appearing at
 all only where the exit creature is one.

 EVERY GRADED ELEMENT IS COMPLETE ON ITS OWN LINE, and that is not a style
 choice. Inside a list literal a trailing binary operator does NOT continue onto
 the next line: `a\n  - b` parses as two elements and `a\n  >= b` reports `if` on
 an integer. Both cost a red line here. Where an expression will not fit, it goes
 into a `let` above the list.

 THE FILL ORDER IS THE ZIG'S: farm animals, then the corner pair, then the ducks.
 The sort that follows is stable, so equal depths keep this order and it is part
 of the answer rather than a convenience.

 We say:

Section: The Reaches

  reach-got : List Integer
  reach-got = [farm-seg-reach, safari-seg-reach]

  reach-want : List Integer
  reach-want = [3, 5]

Section: The Chain

 Six segments, so both cutoffs fall inside it.

  segs : List Segment
  segs = [segment-at 0, segment-at 1, segment-at 2, segment-at 3, segment-at 4, segment-at 5]

  chain : List Integer
  chain = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

  origin : Pose
  origin = Pose { along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, hw = 2.0 }

  farm-at : Integer -> Integer
  farm-at (d) = list-length (seg-farm segs chain origin d 2.0)

  safari-at : Integer -> Integer
  safari-at (d) = list-length (seg-safari segs chain origin d 2.0)

 The farm stops at three and the corner pair carries to five, so entries three
 and four are exactly where the two rules disagree.

  cutoff-got : List Boolean
  cutoff-got =
    [ farm-at 0 > 0,
      farm-at 2 > 0,
      farm-at 3 == 0,
      farm-at 4 == 0,
      farm-at 5 == 0,
      safari-at 3 > 0,
      safari-at 4 > 0,
      safari-at 5 == 0 ]

  cutoff-want : List Boolean
  cutoff-want = [True, True, True, True, True, True, True, True]

 THE TERMINUS HAS NO EXIT CORNER, so it contributes no corner pair however close
 it is -- a separate refusal from running out of reach, and it reaches the same
 empty list.

  term-got : List Integer
  term-got = [list-length (seg-safari [segment-at 18] [0] origin 0 2.0)]

  term-want : List Integer
  term-want = [0]

  term-flag-got : List Boolean
  term-flag-got = [list-length (seg-safari [segment-at 0] [0] origin 0 2.0) > 0]

  term-flag-want : List Boolean
  term-flag-want = [True]

Section: The Ducks

 Only a segment whose exit creature is a pond has any, and segment 12 is the one
 that does.

  duck-count : Integer -> Integer
  duck-count (i) = list-length (seg-ducks [segment-at i] [0] origin 0)

  duck-got : List Boolean
  duck-got =
    [ duck-count 12 > 0,
      duck-count 0 == 0,
      duck-count 4 == 0,
      duck-count 18 == 0 ]

  duck-want : List Boolean
  duck-want = [True, True, True, True]

 Beyond the safari reach there are none even on the pond segment, which is the
 same cutoff the corner pair takes.

  duck-reach-got : List Integer
  duck-reach-got =
    [ list-length (seg-ducks segs chain origin 5),
      list-length (seg-ducks [segment-at 12] [0] origin 0) ]

  duck-reach-want : List Integer
  duck-reach-want = [0, 6]

Section: The Fill Order

 One chain segment contributes its farm animals, then its corner pair, then its
 ducks -- so the total is the three pieces and the pieces come out in that order.

  order-got : List Integer
  order-got =
    let d0 = list-length (seg-billboards segs chain origin 0)
    in let f0 = farm-at 0
    in let s0 = safari-at 0
    in let k0 = list-length (seg-ducks segs chain origin 0)
    in [d0 - (f0 + s0 + k0)]

  order-want : List Integer
  order-want = [0]

 The whole walk is the segments' contributions concatenated, and a chain with
 nothing in it contributes nothing.

  one-seg : List Segment
  one-seg = [segment-at 0]

  walk-got : List Integer
  walk-got =
    [ list-length (walk-billboards segs [] origin 0),
      list-length (walk-billboards one-seg [0] origin 0) - list-length (seg-billboards one-seg [0] origin 0),
      list-length (walk-billboards segs chain origin 6) ]

  walk-want : List Integer
  walk-want = [0, 0, 0]

 It grows as the chain does: a longer chain collects at least what a shorter one
 did, because every entry only adds.

  grow-got : List Boolean
  grow-got =
    let a = list-length (walk-billboards segs chain origin 0)
    in let b = list-length (walk-billboards segs chain origin 1)
    in let c = list-length (walk-billboards segs chain origin 2)
    in [a >= b, b >= c]

  grow-want : List Boolean
  grow-want = [True, True]

Section: Behind Him

 The previous segment's animals are still in view over his shoulder on a corner,
 and the chain cannot reach them -- it starts where he is and only runs forward.

  behind-got : List Boolean
  behind-got =
    [ list-length (behind-billboards segs chain origin 0) >= 0,
      list-length (behind-ducks segs chain origin (segment-at 12)) > 0,
      list-length (behind-ducks segs chain origin (segment-at 0)) == 0 ]

  behind-want : List Boolean
  behind-want = [True, True, True]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cn-reach " reach-got reach-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "cn-cutoff" cutoff-got cutoff-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cn-term  " term-got term-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "cn-tflag " term-flag-got term-flag-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "cn-duck  " duck-got duck-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cn-dreach" duck-reach-got duck-reach-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cn-order " order-got order-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cn-walk  " walk-got walk-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "cn-grow  " grow-got grow-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "cn-behind" behind-got behind-want)
  end
